---
// The first tool page. One URL, indexable, its own title and prose — people arrive here
// from a search for "merge pdf", not from the landing page.
//
// The interactive part is one island. Everything above and below it is static, so the page
// is readable and its claim is checkable with JavaScript switched off.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Merge PDF — combine PDFs on your own computer | burrow"
  description="Combine several PDFs into one, in the order you choose. The files are merged in your browser and never uploaded, because there is no server to upload them to."
>
  <div class="read">
    <h1>Merge PDF</h1>

    <p>
      Combine several PDFs into one file, in whatever order you put them. The merging happens in
      this browser, on this computer — the files are not uploaded, because burrow has no server that
      could receive them.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    /*
    NO `client:*` DIRECTIVE, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE.

    Astro's hydration directives bootstrap an island with an INLINE script, and
    `script-src 'self'` carries no `'unsafe-inline'` and no nonce (ADR 0014). Measured, on
    this page: the browser refuses the bootstrap, the island never hydrates, and the tool
    renders as dead markup — no error anyone would see except a CSP line in the console.

    That is the same wall the engine harness hit, and apps/web/CLAUDE.md records the same
    answer: move to an external file rather than loosen the policy. So the component is
    mounted by the module below, which Astro bundles to `_astro/*.js` and references with a
    `src` — an ordinary same-origin script the policy already permits.

    The ENGINES are still not loaded here. The tool starts a worker the first time a file is
    chosen, which is what "load the wasm module lazily, when the user engages with the tool"
    means. Mounting the component is a few kilobytes; starting the engines is about 420 KB over
    the wire (1.8 MB uncompressed) --- it was 6.8 MB until spike 0004 took PDFium out of it.
  */
  }
  <div id="merge-tool"></div>

  <script>
    import { type Component, mount } from "svelte";

    import MergeTool from "../components/MergeTool.svelte";

    const target = document.getElementById("merge-tool");
    if (target) {
      // The cast is Astro's type, not ours. `@astrojs/svelte` declares a `.svelte` default
      // export as an ASTRO component -- one that takes client directives -- because that is
      // what `<MergeTool client:load />` would need. Mounting it by hand is the same module
      // reached through Svelte's own API instead, and the two declarations do not overlap.
      // `mount` is the only correct call here (see the comment above on `client:*`), so the
      // cast goes where the mismatch is rather than the mount going where the types are.
      mount(MergeTool as unknown as Component, { target });
    }
  </script>

  <noscript>
    <div class="read no-js">
      <h2>This tool needs JavaScript</h2>
      <p>
        The merging is done by code running in your browser, so with JavaScript switched off there
        is nothing to do the work. There is no server-side version to fall back to — that is the
        whole design, not a missing feature.
      </p>
      <p>
        With JavaScript on, this page lets you choose several PDFs, put them in the order you want,
        and download the combined file. Nothing is sent anywhere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </noscript>

  <div class="read">
    <h2>What happens to large files</h2>

    <p>
      Everything is held in this browser tab's memory, so the practical limit is what your device
      can spare rather than an upload size. burrow refuses a set of files totalling more than 512 MB
      before it opens any of them, and refuses an output of more than 10,000 pages before it writes
      any of them — so an oversized job is refused rather than failing part way through and leaving
      you with something incomplete.
    </p>

    <p>
      The size limit is applied to what you chose, not to what has been loaded — burrow adds up the
      file sizes and refuses before it opens the first one, so an oversized selection is turned away
      immediately rather than after it has filled the tab.
    </p>

    <p>
      What that does not save you from, said here rather than discovered: a very large merge that is
      under both limits can still make the tab slow while it runs, and on a phone the browser may
      stop the tab entirely. That is the device's memory ceiling, reached inside the PDF engine
      where nothing can intervene.
    </p>

    <h2>What is kept, and what is not</h2>

    <p>
      Pages, their contents and their sizes come across unchanged. Bookmarks, attachments and form
      fields are kept from the <strong>first</strong> file — later files contribute their pages. Merging
      outlines from several documents has no obviously right answer, so burrow does not guess at one.
    </p>

    <p>
      A file that is encrypted, damaged, or not a PDF stops the whole merge. burrow will not quietly
      leave a file out: a combined document that is missing pages looks complete, and you would find
      out when you needed them.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The no-JS block is ordinary content, but it only ever appears in place of the tool, so
     it gets the same top margin the tool has. */
  .no-js {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
  }
</style>
